"""Character table for the PSP game's text: glyph code -> Unicode character, for READING Japanese blocks
(tools/jpdump.py, the subtitle tools) and for matching the text runs named in script/fill_rules.json (tools/build_text.py).
The build never encodes Japanese text with it.

The PSP's 16-bit character codes are glyph numbers in FONT.BIN (12x12 cells, 21 per texture row; tools/psp_font.py),
not Shift-JIS. The table was worked out from the game itself:
  - kana: from speaker-name anchors, names whose reading is known (Roll = ロール = D4 52 D2, Tron = トロン,
    Bon = ボン, Servbot = コブン, Paprika = パプリカ, ...). They show the kana follow gojuon order, hiragana from 0x54 and
    katakana from 0xA8, skipping every code with code % 21 == 20 (the last cell of each font row is left unused).
  - digits 0x00-0x09, punctuation 0x0A-0x13, upper case from 0x15, lower case from 0x30 (the same codes as the
    English PS1 text uses), full-width space 0x4F, symbols 0xFD-0x10B (button icons etc., see tools/msgtool.py).
  - kanji from 0x111, in no standard order. KANJI below holds the 323 used by the PSP's subtitles, read 2026-09-29
    (docs/RESEARCH.md §11); kanji used only elsewhere are still unmapped and print as {XXX}.
The table is provisional: enough to read the script, not a verified full font map (docs/RESEARCH.md §4)."""
# H / K: hiragana and katakana in the order of the font cells ("〓" marks a cell whose character is not identified)
H='あいうえおかきくけこさしすせそたちつてと' 'なにぬねのはひふへほまみむめもやゆよらり' 'るれろわをん' 'ぁぃぅぇぉっゃゅょ' 'がぎぐげご' 'ざじずぜぞだぢづでどばびぶべぼぱぴぷぺぽ'
K='アイウエオカキクケコサシスセソタチツテト' 'ナニヌネノハヒフヘホマミムメモヤユヨラリ' 'ルレロワヲン' 'ァィゥェォッャュョ' '〓ガギグゲ' 'ゴザジズゼゾダヂヅデドバビブベボパピプペ' 'ポ'
T={}
# assign consecutive codes from start, skipping the unused last cell of each 21-cell font row
def fill(start,s):
    c=start
    for ch in s:
        while c%21==20: c+=1
        T[c]=ch; c+=1
fill(0x54,H); fill(0xA8,K)
for i in range(10): T[i]=str(i)
T.update({0x0A:'、',0x0B:'。',0x0C:"'",0x0D:'！',0x0E:'？',0x0F:'「',0x10:'・',0x11:'(',0x12:')',0x13:'：',0x4F:'　',0x50:'」',0x51:'〜',0x52:'ー'})
import string
fill(0x15,string.ascii_uppercase); fill(0x30,string.ascii_lowercase)

